  "articleBody": "Thinking about making your bathroom more accessible is a smart move, and there are several key areas to focus on to ensure it meets your needs effectively. First off, you'll want to evaluate every obstacle that currently exists. Think about things like high tub ledges, shower curbs, bulky vanities, and even the width of your door. Each of these can create a barrier to safe movement. Once you've identified these, you can start planning for specific changes. A popular and highly effective option is replacing a traditional tub with a barrier-free or curbless shower; this makes entry and exit much safer. Also, consider where grab bars should go and make sure they'll have proper blocking within the wall for secure installation. Accessible sinks and vanities that allow for wheelchair clearance, along with easier-to-use hardware, are also crucial. Remember, if you're aiming for specific accessibility standards, details like door width, turning space, and fixture heights really matter. A professional can help ensure all these elements come together to create a bathroom that's not just accessible, but truly safe and comfortable for you.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}